+      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
+        <span class="section-number">09</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Cumulative mastery</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Mastery means you can reconstruct the system, diagnose a flawed design, verify the runtime, and teach the leverage claim without the chapter open.</p>
+
+        <h3>Round A · rapid retrieval</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative CEO agents retrieval questions">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. State the system’s mission in five words, then name its three nodes.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Uncertainty in, decision out. Brief → deliberation → memo.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Name the three innovations and explain which one enables the other two.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Useful long context, a customizable harness, and compounding domain expertise. Long context is the enabler because the meeting and accumulated expertise create a large information load that must remain usable.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Name four board seats, each seat’s bias, and two tension pairs.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Examples: Revenue—speed and monetization; Compounder—trust and retention; Technical Architect—durability; Moonshot—asymmetric upside. Revenue↔Compounder and Technical↔Product are two tension pairs.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What are the four brief headings and what makes a context file load?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Situation, Stakes, Constraints, Key Question. A sibling file with the <code>.md</code> extension, other than <code>brief.md</code>, is treated as context.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. How are the CEO and board members run differently?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The CEO is the primary Pi agent with an injected prompt. Board members are separate Pi subprocesses with role prompts and persistent session files.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. What do <code>converse</code> and <code>end_deliberation</code> each do?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>converse</code> targets one, several, or all board members and returns responses plus constraint state. <code>end_deliberation</code> collects final positions and hands the CEO the memo-writing step, subject to the early-close guard.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. State the concurrent-round model precisely.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Targets launch concurrently, not as a sequential pipeline of returned answers. Same-round cross-pollination is not ordered or guaranteed; across rounds, members can reliably reread the shared log.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. Name three runtime discrepancies a careful engineer should remember.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Converse is concurrent rather than sequentially fed; Contrarian is not serialized last; and minimum time is enforced by the close guard while minimum budget is not.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. Why can the same brief produce a different vote?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Agent outputs are nondeterministic. The system should preserve evidence and dissent rather than promise a canonical tally.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. Name the core four and one harness-level behavior outside them.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt, and tools. Harness-level examples include input lockdown, subprocess/session orchestration, lifecycle hooks, constraint state, or the TUI.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round B · diagnose the broken system</h3>
+        <ol class="mastery-ladder">
+          <li><div><strong>All seats recommend a cautious pilot.</strong><p>Diagnose likely persona theater. Compare objective functions, time horizons, evidence standards, red lines, and prompts. Add real tension before adding more agents.</p></div></li>
+          <li><div><strong>The brief validates but the memo invents basic facts.</strong><p>Passing headings prove structure, not evidence. Add specific context files, source critical numbers, and trace memo claims to input or label them as generated hypotheses.</p></div></li>
+          <li><div><strong>A learner says the fifth log line read the fourth.</strong><p>Completion order is not an information dependency. Read the scheduling code and inspect message content; use “concurrent within a round, reliably shared across rounds.”</p></div></li>
+          <li><div><strong>The meeting crosses five minutes.</strong><p>Do not assume the limit failed. Check whether a round was in flight, when constraint state was evaluated, whether another <code>converse</code> was blocked, and whether the CEO moved to <code>end_deliberation</code>.</p></div></li>
+          <li><div><strong>The memo is useful, so the team deletes the logs.</strong><p>Protect the full audit trail. Without conversation and tool logs, the team cannot explain claims, inspect dissent, calibrate roles, or improve the system.</p></div></li>
+        </ol>
+
+        <h3>Round C · oral board exam</h3>
+        <div class="teach-back">
+          <p><strong>Five-minute prompt:</strong> “Design a CEO-and-board system for one high-leverage decision. Start with the brief and memo schemas. Choose four seats with conflicting objective functions. Explain the orchestration, context loading, session behavior, constraints, logs, expertise loop, and one skill. State which guarantees live in prompts and which must live in code. End with how you will know the board improved the decision.”</p>
+          <p>Pass when a listener can draw the architecture, predict the artifacts, name the failure modes, and identify the evidence required to audit your claims.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <h3>Mastery rubric</h3>
+        <table class="rubric-table">
+          <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Beginning</th><th>Operational</th><th>Mastery</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td>Contract</td><td>Describes “a board chat”</td><td>Names brief, debate, memo</td><td>Defines validated inputs, reasoning artifacts, and decision schema</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Specialists</td><td>Uses personas</td><td>Uses different roles</td><td>Engineers objective functions, horizons, evidence, and tension pairs</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Runtime</td><td>Trusts UI or docs</td><td>Reads code or logs</td><td>Triangulates prompt intent, code semantics, and runtime evidence</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Constraints</td><td>Uses defaults</td><td>Sets floors and ceilings</td><td>Sizes them to decision value and understands tool-boundary behavior</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Learning</td><td>Keeps the memo</td><td>Keeps logs</td><td>Uses logs to improve roles, prompts, limits, and curated expertise</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Transfer</td><td>Copies Pi details</td><td>Can modify the course system</td><td>Can port the contracts and primitives to another harness</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">10</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Glossary</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Use these terms precisely. A shared vocabulary keeps architecture, prompts, and runtime claims from collapsing into “the agents talked.”</p>
+        <dl class="glossary">
+          <div><dt>CEO &amp; Board</dt><dd>One orchestrator agent plus a configurable set of biased specialist agents that deliberate on a decision.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Brief</dt><dd>The validated input directory: <code>brief.md</code> with Situation, Stakes, Constraints, and Key Question, plus optional sibling Markdown context.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Deliberation</dt><dd>The bounded multi-round workflow in which the CEO frames, broadcasts, targets tensions, watches constraints, and closes.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Memo</dt><dd>The durable decision output containing recommendation, stances, dissent, risks, trade-offs, and next actions.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Multi-agent orchestration</dt><dd>One control node coordinating several agent executions, their state, messages, tools, and outputs.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Adversarial pattern</dt><dd>Controlled disagreement among specialists whose objective functions expose different classes of error.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Tension pair</dt><dd>Two roles whose biases illuminate a recurring trade-off, such as near-term revenue versus long-term trust.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>converse</code></dt><dd>The CEO tool that calls named, multiple, or all active board members and returns responses plus constraint state.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>end_deliberation</code></dt><dd>The closing tool that gathers final statements and moves the CEO to memo synthesis.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Constraint engine</dt><dd>The logic that calculates elapsed time, cost, warning thresholds, and maximum-hit state around board calls.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agent harness</dt><dd>The runtime layer exposing system-prompt control, tools, lifecycle hooks, sessions, subprocesses or subagents, and UI behavior.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Persistent session</dt><dd>A per-board-member Pi session file reused during one deliberation so later calls retain that member’s context.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Expertise</dt><dd>Persistent, role-scoped memory and patterns around a specific domain, curated across deliberations.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Observability</dt><dd>The logs, costs, messages, tool records, sessions, and artifacts needed to explain and improve a run.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Core four</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, and tools—the four primary levers of an agentic system.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>One-shot system</dt><dd>A workflow with a deliberately narrow interaction surface: start, stop, or wrap rather than arbitrary chat.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agent model diversity</dt><dd>Assigning different model families to seats to add another source of perspective, after role diversity is well designed.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agentic engineering</dt><dd>Building agent systems that solve problems and make decisions, not only generate code.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+      </section>
+
+      <footer class="chapter-footer">
+        <nav class="chapter-footer__nav" aria-label="Chapter navigation">
+          <a href="../index.html">← Book hub</a>
+          <span>Next · Lead Agents (pending)</span>
+        </nav>
+      </footer>
+    </main>
+  </div>
+
+  <script>
+    (() => {
+      const topBar = document.getElementById('top-progress');
+      const railBar = document.getElementById('rail-progress');
+      const label = document.getElementById('progress-label');
+      const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-nav a')];
+      const sections = links.map(link => document.querySelector(link.getAttribute('href'))).filter(Boolean);
+      let progressFramePending = false;
+
+      const updateProgress = () => {
+        if (!topBar || !railBar || !label) return;
+        const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
+        const value = max > 0 ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, window.scrollY / max)) : 0;
+        const percent = `${Math.round(value * 100)}%`;
+        topBar.style.width = percent;
+        railBar.style.width = percent;
+        label.textContent = percent;
+      };
+
+      const requestProgressUpdate = () => {
+        if (progressFramePending) return;
+        progressFramePending = true;
+        requestAnimationFrame(() => {
+          updateProgress();
+          progressFramePending = false;
+        });
+      };
+
+      if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
+        const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
+          const visible = entries
+            .filter(entry => entry.isIntersecting)
+            .sort((a, b) => b.intersectionRatio - a.intersectionRatio)[0];
+          if (!visible) return;
+          links.forEach(link => {
+            const active = link.getAttribute('href') === `#${visible.target.id}`;
+            if (active) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
+            else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
+          });
+        }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -65% 0px', threshold: [0, .1, .5] });
+        sections.forEach(section => observer.observe(section));
+      }
+
+      updateProgress();
+      window.addEventListener('scroll', requestProgressUpdate, { passive: true });
+      window.addEventListener('resize', requestProgressUpdate);
+    })();
+  </script>
+</body>
+</html>
